  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 425 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over southern Alberta with\nhigh over the upper Midwest into the Northern Plains. Upper\nlevel analysis places broad trough over the north central CONUS,\nwith high over the Desert Southwest into the Southern Plains and\nridging over the West Coast. Quiet weather remains over our \narea, with temperatures mainly in the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nFor today, ridging enhances over the West Coast while warmer air\nstarts to return into our area. Temperatures climb back to near\naverage over most locations while dry weather continues.\n\nOn Thursday, temperatures continue to warm, with highs climbing\ninto the mid 80s to mid 90s before a modest cold front drops\ninto the area late in the day into the evening. Most areas will\ncontinue to remain dry, but latest guidance indicates a few \nlate night rain showers could develop over the southern James \nRiver Valley. With that said, a potent little short wave passes \njust north of the International Border, and if that feature \ndrops further south than currently forecast, it could result in\nsome precipitation around the Turtle Mountains. Will have to \nkeep an eye on this.\n\nTemperatures will be a bit cooler behind the front, but remain\nseasonal through the remainder of the week while dry weather\ncontinues. Low to medium chances (20 to 40 percent) for \nprecipitation return next week as a variety of modest waves are\nexpected to pass through modestly cyclonic flow aloft.",
